Memory: O(n + m) for a directed graph, O(n + 2m) for an undirected one, because each undirected
edge is stored twice. Iterate neighbours of u: O(deg(u)), which is optimal. Check whether edge
(u,v) exists: O(deg(u)), which is slow. If a problem asks that repeatedly , use aset or a matrix.
2.1.1 The vector version, for multiple test cases
Codeforces problems very often have t test ca

a problem asks that repeatedly , use aset or a matrix.
2.1.1 The vector version, for multiple test cases
Codeforces problems very often have t test cases in one input. Global arrays then have to be cleared,
and clearing all N entries per test is O(N) per test, which times out. Two safe options:
// Option A: local vector, sized per test. Cleanest and safe.
int n, m;
cin >> n >> m;
vector<vector<int>> adj(n + 1);
// Option B: global array, but clear only what you used
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) adj[i].clear();
Option A is what you should default to. It costs a little allocation time and saves you from the classic
“passes sample, fails test 2” bug where leftover edges from the previous test case are still in the lists.
2.1.2 Weighted graphs
vector<pair<int,int>> adj[N]; // (neighbour, weigh

y algorithm
O(n2) instead of O(n + m).
Use a matrix when: n <= about 500, or the problem is naturally dense, or you are running Floyd-
Warshall (Part 11), which needs a matrix anyway .
For weighted matrices, store the weight and use a large sentinel for “no edge”:
const int INF = 1e9;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
g[i][j] = (i == j ? 0 : INF);
2.3 Edge list
Just store the edges.
struct Edge { int u, v, w; };
vector<Edge> edges;
edges.push_back({u, v, w});
Useless for traversal, because finding the neighbours of a vertex means scanning everything. Perfect
for two specific algorithms:
• Kruskal’s MST (Part 10), which sorts all edges by weight.
• Bellman-Ford (Part 11), which relaxes every edge n - 1 times.
Also handy when a problem asks you to answer something abo

on.
