Part 2
Representing Graphs in C++
Adjacency lists, matrices, edge lists, grids
In the trees course a node was a struct with two pointers, and the structure was the memory layout.
Graphs are different. There is no GraphNode. A graph is stored as a table of who is next to whom, and
you get to choose the table.
There are three standard choices. In competitive programming you will use the first one ninety percent
of the time.
2.1 Adjacency list
For every vertex, store the list of its neighbours.
G1:
1: 2 4
2: 1 3 4 5
3: 2 6
4: 1 2 5
5: 2 4
6: 3
7: 8
8: 7
const int N = 200005;
vector<int> adj[N];
int n, m;
int main() {
cin >> n >> m;
for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
int u, v;
cin >> u >> v;
adj[u].push_back(v);
adj[v].push_back(u); // undirected: BOTH directions
}
}

cin >> n >> m;
for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
int u, v;
cin >> u >> v;
adj[u].push_back(v);
adj[v].push_back(u); // undirected: BOTH directions
}
}
For a directed graph, delete the second push_back. That single line is the whole difference, and
forgetting it is the most common bug in this entire course.
Iterating over the neighbours of u:
16

Representing Graphs in C++ 17
for (int v : adj[u]) {
// v is a neighbour of u
}
Memory: O(n + m) for a directed graph, O(n + 2m) for an undirected one, because each undirected
edge is stored twice. Iterate neighbours of u: O(deg(u)), which is optimal. Check whether edge
(u,v) exists: O(deg(u)), which is slow. If a problem asks that repeatedly , use aset or a matrix.
2.1.1 The vector version, for multiple test cases
Codeforces problems very often have t test cases in one input. Global arrays then have to be cleared,
and clearing all N entries per test is O(N) per test, which times out. Two safe options:
// Option A: local vector, sized per test. Cleanest and safe.
int n, m;
cin >> n >> m;
vector<vector<int>> adj(n + 1);
// Option B: global array, but clear only what you used

int n, m;
cin >> n >> m;
vector<vector<int>> adj(n + 1);
// Option B: global array, but clear only what you used
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) adj[i].clear();
Option A is what you should default to. It costs a little allocation time and saves you from the classic
“passes sample, fails test 2” bug where leftover edges from the previous test case are still in the lists.
2.1.2 Weighted graphs
vector<pair<int,int>> adj[N]; // (neighbour, weight)
adj[u].push_back({v, w});
adj[v].push_back({u, w});
for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) { ... } // C++17
for (auto p : adj[u]) { int v = p.first, w = p.second; ... } // any version
Keep the pair as(neighbour, weight), not (weight, neighbour). Dijkstra’s priority queue wants

For weighted matrices, store the weight and use a large sentinel for “no edge”:
const int INF = 1e9;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
g[i][j] = (i == j ? 0 : INF);
2.3 Edge list
Just store the edges.
struct Edge { int u, v, w; };
vector<Edge> edges;
edges.push_back({u, v, w});
Useless for traversal, because finding the neighbours of a vertex means scanning everything. Perfect
for two specific algorithms:
• Kruskal’s MST (Part 10), which sorts all edges by weight.
• Bellman-Ford (Part 11), which relaxes every edge n - 1 times.
Also handy when a problem asks you to answer something about edges themselves, such as “delete
edge i and report the number of components”.
