Part 2
Representing Graphs in C++
Adjacency lists, matrices, edge lists, grids
In the trees course a node was a struct with two pointers, and the structure was the memory layout.
Graphs are different. There is no GraphNode. A graph is stored as a table of who is next to whom, and
you get to choose the table.
There are three standard choices. In competitive programming you will use the first one ninety percent
of the time.
2.1 Adjacency list
For every vertex, store the list of its neighbours.
G1:
1: 2 4
2: 1 3 4 5
3: 2 6
4: 1 2 5
5: 2 4
6: 3
7: 8
8: 7
const int N = 200005;
vector<int> adj[N];
int n, m;
int main() {
cin >> n >> m;
for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
int u, v;
cin >> u >> v;
adj[u].push_back(v);
adj[v].push_back(u); // undirected: BOTH directions
}
}
For a directed graph, delete the second push_back. That single line is the whole difference, and
forgetting it is the most common bug in this entire course.
Iterating over the neighbours of u:
16

--- Page Break ---

Representing Graphs in C++ 17
for (int v : adj[u]) {
// v is a neighbour of u
}
Memory: O(n + m) for a directed graph, O(n + 2m) for an undirected one, because each undirected
edge is stored twice. Iterate neighbours of u: O(deg(u)), which is optimal. Check whether edge
(u,v) exists: O(deg(u)), which is slow. If a problem asks that repeatedly , use aset or a matrix.
2.1.1 The vector version, for multiple test cases
Codeforces problems very often have t test cases in one input. Global arrays then have to be cleared,
and clearing all N entries per test is O(N) per test, which times out. Two safe options:
// Option A: local vector, sized per test. Cleanest and safe.
int n, m;
cin >> n >> m;
vector<vector<int>> adj(n + 1);
// Option B: global array, but clear only what you used
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) adj[i].clear();
Option A is what you should default to. It costs a little allocation time and saves you from the classic
“passes sample, fails test 2” bug where leftover edges from the previous test case are still in the lists.
2.1.2 Weighted graphs
vector<pair<int,int>> adj[N]; // (neighbour, weight)
adj[u].push_back({v, w});
adj[v].push_back({u, w});
for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) { ... } // C++17
for (auto p : adj[u]) { int v = p.first, w = p.second; ... } // any version
Keep the pair as(neighbour, weight), not (weight, neighbour). Dijkstra’s priority queue wants
the opposite order, and mixing the two conventions in one file is a reliable way to lose forty minutes.
2.2 Adjacency matrix
A 2D array where g[u][v] is 1 if the edge exists.
G1 as a matrix (rows and columns 1..8):
1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8
1 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0
2 1 0 1 1 1 0 0 0
3 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0
4 1 1 0 0 1 0 0 0
5 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0
6 0 0 1 0 0 0 0 0
7 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 1

--- Page Break ---

Representing Graphs in C++ 18
8 0 0 0 0 0 0 1 0
const int MAXN = 505;
int g[MAXN][MAXN];
g[u][v] = 1;
g[v][u] = 1; // undirected
Notice the matrix of an undirected graph is symmetric, and its diagonal is 0 for a simple graph.
Memory: O(n 2). With n = 500 that is 250000 ints, about 1 MB. Fine. With n = 10 5 it is 10 10
ints. Impossible. Check whether edge (u,v) exists: O(1). This is its only real advantage. Iterate
neighbours of u: O(n), even if u has one neighbour. On a sparse graph this makes every algorithm
O(n2) instead of O(n + m).
Use a matrix when: n <= about 500, or the problem is naturally dense, or you are running Floyd-
Warshall (Part 11), which needs a matrix anyway .
For weighted matrices, store the weight and use a large sentinel for “no edge”:
const int INF = 1e9;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
g[i][j] = (i == j ? 0 : INF);
2.3 Edge list
Just store the edges.
struct Edge { int u, v, w; };
vector<Edge> edges;
edges.push_back({u, v, w});
Useless for traversal, because finding the neighbours of a vertex means scanning everything. Perfect
for two specific algorithms:
• Kruskal’s MST (Part 10), which sorts all edges by weight.
• Bellman-Ford (Part 11), which relaxes every edge n - 1 times.
Also handy when a problem asks you to answer something about edges themselves, such as “delete
edge i and report the number of components”.
2.4 Comparison
Adjacency list Adjacency matrix Edge list
Memory O(n + m) O(n 2) O(m)
Is (u,v) an edge? O(deg u) O(1) O(m)
Loop over neighbours of u O(deg u) O(n) O(m)
Add an edge O(1) O(1) O(1)

--- Page Break ---

Representing Graphs in C++ 19
Adjacency list Adjacency matrix Edge list
Good for almost everything dense, small n, Floyd Kruskal, Bellman-Ford
Default to the adjacency list. Switch only for a stated reason.
2.5 Reading input, the four standard shapes
// 1. undirected, unweighted
cin >> n >> m;
vector<vector<int>> adj(n + 1);
for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
int u, v; cin >> u >> v;
adj[u].push_back(v);
adj[v].push_back(u);
}
// 2. directed, unweighted
for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
int u, v; cin >> u >> v;
adj[u].push_back(v);
}
// 3. undirected, weighted
vector<vector<pair<int,int>>> adj(n + 1);
for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
int u, v, w; cin >> u >> v >> w;
adj[u].push_back({v, w});
adj[v].push_back({u, w});
}
// 4. a tree given as parent array (very common)
// p[i] is the parent of vertex i, for i = 2..n
vector<vector<int>> adj(n + 1);
for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
int p; cin >> p;
adj[p].push_back(i);
adj[i].push_back(p);
}
Shape 4 deserves attention. Many Codeforces problems hand you a tree as n - 1 parent values rather
than edges. Build it as an ordinary undirected graph anyway , then root it at 1 during the DFS. Part 12
explains why that is better than trying to use the parent array directly .
2.5.1 Fast input
With m up to 2 * 105 or more, cin without this line is genuinely slow enough to fail:
ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
cin.tie(nullptr);
Put those two lines at the top of main in every solution. They cost nothing and save you from a class

--- Page Break ---

Representing Graphs in C++ 20
of time limit failures that have nothing to do with your algorithm.
2.6 Grids: the graph you never build
A grid problem gives you something like
4 5
..#..
.##..
.....
##.#.
Every free cell is a vertex, and each cell is joined to the free cells directly above, below, left and right.
There are up to 4 * r * c edges, and writing them into an adjacency list is a waste of memory and
typing. Instead, generate neighbours on demand:
int R, C;
vector<string> g;
int dx[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
int dy[4] = { 0, 0,-1, 1};
// neighbours of cell (x, y):
for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
int nx = x + dx[k], ny = y + dy[k];
if (nx < 0 || nx >= R || ny < 0 || ny >= C) continue; // off the board
if (g[nx][ny] == '#') continue; // wall
// (nx, ny) is a neighbour
}
Everything in this course works on grids with that loop substituted for for (int v : adj[u]). BFS
on a grid is BFS. Components on a grid are components. Nothing new is happening.
For 8 directions:
int dx[8] = {-1,-1,-1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1};
int dy[8] = {-1, 0, 1,-1, 1,-1, 0, 1};
If you ever need a single integer id for a cell, use id = x * C + y, and back again with x = id / C,
y = id % C. That is needed for DSU on a grid, and occasionally for a visited set.
Order matters for readability, not correctness. Any order of the four directions gives the same BFS
distances.
2.7 Storing visited and distance arrays
vector<int> vis(n + 1, 0);
vector<int> dist(n + 1, -1); // -1 means "not reached"
vector<int> par(n + 1, 0); // for path reconstruction
vector<long long> d(n + 1, 1e18); // for Dijkstra

--- Page Break ---

Representing Graphs in C++ 21
Two notes that will save you real debugging time:
• Size everything n + 1 when vertices are 1-indexed. Sizing it n and then touching vis[n] is
undefined behaviour that usually looks like a wrong answer, not a crash.
• Prefer dist initialised to -1 over a separate visited array for BFS. One array , one meaning: “-1
means not visited”. Fewer things to forget.
2.8 Memory arithmetic, done once
int is 4 bytes, long long is 8 bytes
vector<int> adj[200005] with 2m = 4*10^5 total entries
~ 1.6 MB of data plus vector overhead, comfortable
int g[500][500] = 1 MB fine
int g[5000][5000] = 100 MB usually too much
int g[100000][100000] impossible
Typical Codeforces memory limit: 256 MB
2.9 Common mistakes
• Pushing an undirected edge once. Say it with me: both directions.
• Not clearing global adjacency lists between test cases, or clearing all N of them and timing out.
• Sizing arrays n instead of n + 1 .
• Using an adjacency matrix on a sparse graph with large n.
• Storing (weight, neighbour) in one place and (neighbour, weight) in another.
• Forgetting fast IO and losing to the input reader rather than the algorithm.
• Rebuilding a grid into an explicit adjacency list. Works, wastes memory and time, and adds
bugs.
• Forgetting the bounds check before reading g[nx][ny]. In C++ that reads out of bounds
instead of throwing, so the failure is silent.
2.10 Check yourself
1. Why is an adjacency list preferred over a matrix when n = 10 5 and m = 2 * 105?
2. What is the memory cost of the adjacency list of an undirected graph with m edges, and why is it
not O(m)?
3. Which two algorithms actually prefer an edge list?
4. Write the input loop for a directed weighted graph from memory .
5. On a grid, what does id = x * C + y accomplish, and when do you need it?
6. A problem has t test cases with total n up to 2 * 10 5. What is wrong with clearing a global
adj[200005] in full at the start of each test?
7. Which representation makes “is there an edge between u and v” O(1), and what do you pay for it?

--- Page Break ---

Part 5
Connected Components and Flood Fill
The first real application of DFS and BFS
5.1 The pattern
A connected component is a maximal set of vertices that can all reach each other. In G1 there are
two: {1,2,3,4,5,6} and {7,8}.
The pattern for anything about components is four lines you already saw:
int comps = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
if (!vis[i]) {
comps++;
dfs(i); // or bfs(i)
}
}
Every time the loop finds an unvisited vertex, that vertex belongs to a component nobody has touched
yet, so we increment and explore all of it. When the loop ends, every vertex has been visited exactly
once and comps is the answer.
Use DFS or BFS, it makes no difference to the answer. DFS is two lines shorter, BFS is safe on very
deep graphs.
Time O(n + m). Space O(n).
5.2 Labelling every vertex with its component
Counting is rarely enough. Usually you want to know which component a vertex is in, and how big
each one is.
vector<int> comp; // comp[v] = component id of v, 0 means unassigned
vector<int> compSize; // compSize[c] = number of vertices in component c
void dfs(int u, int c, vector<vector<int>>& adj) {
comp[u] = c;
compSize[c]++;
for (int v : adj[u])
if (comp[v] == 0)
dfs(v, c, adj);
}
35

