Connected Components and Flood Fill 36
int main() {
// ... read the graph ...
comp.assign(n + 1, 0);
compSize.assign(1, 0); // index 0 unused
int c = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
if (comp[i] == 0) {
c++;
compSize.push_back(0);
dfs(i, c, adj);
}
}
}
Now these questions are all O(1) or O(n):
Are u and v connected? comp[u] == comp[v]
How many components? c
Size of the largest component? max over compSize
Is the graph connected? c == 1
On G1: comp = [_,1,1,1,1,1,1,2,2], compSize = [_,6,2], so the graph is not connected and the
largest component has 6 vertices.
5.2.1 Two facts you will reuse
1. To connect a graph with c components you need at least c - 1 extra edges. Join them in a
chain. This is a very common one-line answer in Div 2 A and B problems.
2. A component with k vertices and exactly k - 1 edges is a tree. More edges means it contains
a cycle. Counting edges per component is a cheap way to detect that (Codeforces 977E is exactly
this).
To count edges inside a component while doing DFS, add up the degrees of its vertices and divide by
2.
5.3 Flood fill on a grid
The grid version of the same pattern, and the reason the technique is called flood fill: you pour paint
on a cell and it spreads to everything connected.
G4:
. . # . .
. # # . .
. . . . .
# # . # .
How many separate regions of . are there? Run flood fill from each unvisited free cell.
int R, C;

--- Page Break ---

Connected Components and Flood Fill 37
vector<string> g;
vector<vector<bool>> vis;
int dx[4] = {-1,1,0,0}, dy[4] = {0,0,-1,1};
int fill(int x, int y) { // returns the area of this region
vis[x][y] = true;
int area = 1;
for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
int nx = x + dx[k], ny = y + dy[k];
if (nx < 0 || nx >= R || ny < 0 || ny >= C) continue;
if (g[nx][ny] == '#' || vis[nx][ny]) continue;
area += fill(nx, ny);
}
return area;
}
int main() {
// read R, C, g
vis.assign(R, vector<bool>(C, false));
int regions = 0, best = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < R; i++)
for (int j = 0; j < C; j++)
if (g[i][j] == '.' && !vis[i][j]) {
regions++;
best = max(best, fill(i, j));
}
cout << regions << " " << best << "\n";
}
G4 has 20 cells, 6 of them walls, so 14 free cells. Every free cell turns out to be reachable from every
other, so the answer is one region of area 14.
Now turn cell (2,2) into a wall as well:
. . # . .
. # # . .
. . # . .
# # . # .
The board splits into three regions: the left block {(0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(2,0),(2,1)} of size 5, the
right block of size 7, and the single cell (3,2), which is now walled in on all four sides. Trace that by
hand before moving on. Isolated cells are exactly the case people forget.
Complexity O(R * C), since each cell is entered once and does O(4) work. In graph terms n = R*C
and m <= 2*R*C, so this is the usual O(n + m).
5.3.1 Recursion depth on grids
A 1000 by 1000 grid of free cells means up to 10 6 nested calls in the worst case (a snake-shaped
corridor). That will overflow the stack on many judges. For large grids, write flood fill with an explicit
BFS queue instead. The code is the grid BFS from Part 4 with the distance array replaced by a visited
array . This is a real failure mode, not a theoretical one.

--- Page Break ---

Connected Components and Flood Fill 38
5.4 Variations you should be able to write immediately
count components counter around the loop
size of each component accumulate during DFS
largest component max of the sizes
are u and v connected compare component ids
number of edges to make it connected (number of components) - 1
