For the “are u and v connected” question asked many times, and especially when edges are being
added over time, Part 9 (DSU) is the better tool. DFS answers it once in O(n + m); DSU answers it in
near O(1) per query and supports adding edges online.
5.5 Common mistakes
• Starting DFS only from vertex 1. The classic. Loop over all vertices.
• Counting components on a directed graph with this method. Plain DFS on a directed graph
does not give components in any meaningful sense. What you want there is either “weakly
connected” (ignore directions and use this method) or strongly connected components (Part 13).
Read the problem carefully .
• Forgetting that isolated vertices are components too. A vertex with degree 0 is its own
component, and problems test that.
• Grid flood fill by recursion on a 106 cell grid. Use BFS.
• Marking vis only on the starting cell and forgetting it inside the loop, so cells are pushed
repeatedly .
• Reading the grid with cin >> g[i] but forgetting to size the vector first.
5.6 Codeforces problems that test exactly this
• 217A, Ice Skating. Components on points that share a row or column. https://codeforces.com/pro
blemset/problem/217/A
• 977E, Cyclic Components. Component that is exactly a cycle: every vertex has degree 2. https:
//codeforces.com/problemset/problem/977/E
• 445B, DZY Loves Chemistry . Components, and the answer is a power of two. https://codeforces.c
om/problemset/problem/445/B
• 1176E, Cover it! Colour by BFS layer parity and pick the smaller class. https://codeforces.com/pro
blemset/problem/1176/E
